 "customerTurns": 4,
 "transcript": [
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "my hoover's stopped working"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is the drive belt. Switch it on with the head tilted so you can see the bar. Is the brush bar spinning now, or still not?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "its not doing anything at all, no motor noise. the whole thing is dead when i switch it on"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is the switch, cable or motor inside. Try the socket with something else, and check the switch on the vacuum. If the plug has a fuse (UK plugs do), a 13A fuse can be swapped — unplugged. Look along the cable for cuts or damage too. Was it the socket, switch or fuse (and is it sorted), is the cable or plug damaged, or is the supply fine?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "the lamp works in the socket so thats fine. theres black tape on the wire near the plug, my son did it ages ago. its a bit bent there. i havent got a screwdriver for the fuse"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "From what you have described, the most likely cause is a damaged mains cable or plug. Try switching it on again. Does it run now, or is it still dead?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "its still dead. nothing happens when i switch it on. once it came on for a second when i moved the wire but it went off again and i wont do that again, it frightened me"
  },
  {
   "role": "assistant",
   "text": "⚠ Stop using this appliance\nStop using it now. If you can do it safely, switch it off at the socket or at the consumer unit (fuse box). A shock usually means an earth or insulation fault, so don't use it again until a qualified electrician or appliance engineer has found and fixed it. From what you have checked, this needs an appliance engineer to look at it: the next steps are beyond the checks that are safe to do yourself."
  }
 ],
